{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1164 he was captured at the Battle of Harim and held prisoner by Nur ad-Din for almost ten years. During this time he was succeeded as count by his cousin Bertrand of Toulouse. Raymond was released in 1173 after a ransom was paid and Bertrand was killed in 1173.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nUpon his return Raymond resumed his rule over Tripoli and also served as regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1184 to 1185 during the minority of Baldwin IV and Baldwin V. As regent he worked to strengthen the kingdom against Saladin. He supported the marriage of Sybilla to Guy of Lusignan in 1180 which later proved controversial. Raymond also negotiated truces with Saladin though these were often broken. In 1185 he arranged the coronation of Baldwin V and became his regent once more after Baldwin IV died in 1186. He attempted to prevent Guy of Lusignan from gaining power but was ultimately unsuccessful.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Crusader states for decades. He was a leading figure among the barons who opposed the rise of Guy of Lusignan. In 1187 he joined forces with Saladin in an attempt to halt Guy’s forces at the Battle of Hattin but the crusaders were decisively defeated. Raymond barely escaped the battle and died shortly after in Tripoli. His death marked the end of an era as the crusader states faced increasing pressure from Saladin. Raymond’s legacy includes his long regency and efforts to maintain stability though his support for Guy of Lusi